    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5490449/assistant-provost-academic-budget-planning": {
      "title": "Assistant Provost Academic Budget Planning",
      "description": "Assistant Provost Academic Budget Planning Salary $161,700.00 Annually Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Full-Time Administrator Job Number 202600075 Org Office of the Provost Opening Date 09/22/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary The Assistant Provost for Academic Planning is accountable for the integrated budget planning and financial management of an assigned portfolio of colleges, schools, and academic units. The Assistant Provost owns the financial planning relationship with assigned Deans, sets the strategic direction for the portfolio within division standards, and recommends significant portfolio actions to the Associate Provost. This position exercises budget authority within approved parameters and leads school-level planning related to operating budgets, faculty and staff planning, compensation, restricted funds, endowments, capital implications, program financial analysis, assessment integration, accreditation considerations, and pre-award grants planning. The Assistant Provost supervises and develops the Director of Academic Budget Planning, who provides analytical, modeling, reporting, and documentation support for the portfolio. The Assistant Provost remains ultimately accountable for portfolio recommendations, Dean-level advising, and escalation of material issues. Job Responsibilities Operating Budget, Faculty Planning, and Portfolio Management · Lead annual operating budget development for assigned schools and units, including revenue and expenditure planning, faculty and staff assumptions, compensation costs, program activity, strategic investments, and institutional budget parameters. · Monitor budget performance through budget-to-actual review, variance analysis, root-cause assessment, and corrective action planning. · Develop multi-year financial projections incorporating staffing, compensation, program activity, enrollment trends, restricted fund use, and strategic investment scenarios. · Review and approve budget transfers, reallocations, and position changes within assigned portfolio parameters. · Evaluate new, replacement, or restructured faculty and staff position requests for affordability, timing, financial impact, instructional need, and alignment with school and institutional priorities. · Coordinate salary and compensation planning, including merit, equity, market, promotional, overload, adjunct, summer, leave, sabbatical, course buyout, reassigned time, and grant release-time costs. · Conduct salary savings and resource availability analyses and translate findings into reallocation recommendations. · Ensure faculty workload, adjunct staffing, compensation plans, academic personnel costs, and operating budgets are fiscally sound and aligned with institutional policies. Endowments, Restricted Funds, Capital Planning, and Strategic Resource Use · Manage endowment, designated, and restricted fund planning for assigned schools in coordination with appropriate University offices. · Maintain fund status reports, statements of change, spend-down plans, and analyses of available balances. · Advise Deans on strategic use of endowment distributions, designated funds, restricted funds, and start-up funds while ensuring compliance with donor intent and institutional policy. · Integrate capital planning implications into school operating budgets and multi-year financial plans. · Identify opportunities to use restricted, designated, or available funds strategically to support academic priorities and offset operating budget pressure where allowable. Program, Initiative, and Academic Investment Analysis · Serve as the primary financial advisor to assigned Deans, leading portfolio discussions and providing data-informed recommendations on budget performance, fund balances, staffing plans, program investments, operating risks, academic demand, and strategic resource decisions. · Conduct program-level financial analyses, including revenue, expense, ROI, P&L, credit hour production, staffing costs, space, technology, overhead, and other operating costs. · Evaluate proposed academic initiatives through pro forma modeling, scenario analysis, and sustainability review. · Incorporate academic demand, enrollment trends, retention, completion, market indicators, assessment findings, accreditation requirements, mission considerations, and strategic priorities into financial recommendations. · Identify programs or initiatives that are financially strong, marginal, or unsustainable and advise Deans on investment, restructuring, partnership, phase-out consideration, or other action. · Analyze cross-subsidization patterns, operational efficiencies, shared service opportunities, and potential duplication of resources. · Track approved initiatives and implemented efficiencies against original projections and identify risks or corrective actions. Assessment Process Oversight and Academic Planning Integration · Serve as the school-level operational partner to the Associate Provost for Academic Assessment and Accreditation for assigned schools. · Support implementation of institutional assessment standards, timelines, and documentation expectations at the portfolio level. · Advise Program Directors and academic leaders on assessment plan quality, including learning outcomes, measures, targets, collection timelines, findings, and action plans. · Represent the assigned portfolio on the University Assessment Committee or related assessment and accreditation working groups. · Identify and communicate assessment concerns, accreditation risks, or documentation gaps to the Dean and appropriate OASPA leadership. · Participate in annual strategic reviews that connect assessment findings, academic quality indicators, program review results, and budget planning decisions. · Ensure assessment and accreditation findings are incorporated into academic planning and resource discussions where appropriate. Pre-Award Grants Planning and Budget Integration · Serve as the school-level operational partner to the Associate Provost for Grants and Sponsored Research for assigned schools. · Support pre-award grant planning by reviewing budget sustainability, cost-share commitments, faculty workload implications, and post-award financial obligations. · Ensure grant-funded positions, course buyouts, faculty release time, cost-share commitments, and related budget impacts are accurately reflected in school operating plans. · Notify the Associate Provost for Grants and Sponsored Research of financial constraints, sustainability concerns, or institutional commitments that may affect proposal feasibility. · Coordinate with portfolio teams, Deans, faculty, and relevant administrative offices to ensure grant activity is integrated into academic and financial planning. · Support planning for the transition from proposal approval to award implementation, in coordination with Business Affairs and other responsible offices. Director Supervision and Cross-Training · Supervise, mentor, and evaluate the assigned Director of Academic Budget Planning. · Review Director work products and ensure analyses, reports, dashboards, and planning materials meet division standards. · Provide coaching to strengthen the Director’s analytical, technical, communication, and strategic advisory skills. · Participate in OASPA’s cross-training strategy to develop working knowledge of portfolios beyond assigned schools. · Support onboarding and development of Directors and incoming Assistant Provosts. Competencies, Education, Experience Required Qualifications · Master’s degree in higher education administration, finance, business, public administration, public policy, or related field or equivalent work experience. · Minimum of 7 years of progressively responsible experience in academic budget planning, institutional finance, higher education administration, resource planning, or related work. · Demonstrated expertise in operating budget development, multi-year financial modeling, salary planning, position authorization, variance analysis, and resource allocation. · Experience advising Deans or senior academic leaders. · Demonstrated ability to exercise budget authority and make sound financial planning decisions. · Supervisory or mentorship experience with analytical professional staff. · Excellent analytical, communication, relationship-building, and strategic thinking skills. Preferred Qualifications · Doctoral degree or advanced coursework in a relevant field. · Experience in a Provost’s Office, Academic Affairs, academic budget office, or central university planning environment. · Experience with FLAC, endowment and restricted fund administration, capital planning, assessment systems, accreditation processes, and pre-award grants planning. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. St. John’s offers an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, employees can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstore, dining facilities, campus recreation, and sporting events. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5413684/director-of-recruitment-and-student-employment": {
      "title": "Director of Recruitment and Student Employment",
      "description": "Director of Recruitment and Student Employment Salary $150,000.00 - $160,000.00 Annually Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Full-Time Administrator Job Number 202600028 Org Human Resources Opening Date 07/16/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary The Director of Recruitment and Student Employment reports to the Associate Vice President for Human Resources and provides strategic leadership for the University’s recruitment and student employment functions. This position is responsible for modernizing and standardizing recruitment practices, centralizing onboarding, and ensuring compliance with employment regulations. In addition, the Director will play a critical role in implementing recruitment and onboarding functions of the University’s new HR technology platform (NEOED) and driving process improvements aligned with the University’s strategic plan. Job Responsibilities Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions. Direct recruitment operations by maintaining visibility into all open requisitions, monitoring hiring activity, forecasting talent needs, and ensuring recruiters meet established service-level and hiring targets. Lead full life-cycle recruitment of all staff and administrative positions including full-time, part-time, and temporary roles, partnering with academic and administrative leaders to attract, assess, and hire high-caliber talent that supports institutional priorities. Direct oversight of the student employment function, including work-study and non-work study student positions, as well as graduate assistantships across academic and operational units. Centralize and standardize onboarding processes to ensure a consistent, high-quality experience for all new employees, including development of onboarding plans and resources. Drive implementation and optimization of NEOED, including configuration of recruitment and onboarding workflows, training for hiring managers, and change management strategies. Develop and enforce standardized hiring practices across the University; design and deliver training programs for hiring managers to ensure compliance and best practices. Manage immigration sponsorship and compliance for faculty and administrators, including visa processing and related documentation. Continually review recruitment strategies to position the University as an employer of choice and attract diverse, talented applicant pools. Collaborate with hiring managers to identify outreach and sourcing initiatives; oversee internal and external advertising strategies. Serve as the HR liaison and/or sit on search committees for executive-level searches. Negotiate offers on behalf of hiring managers, considering market data and internal equity. Maintain compliance with employment laws and communicate updates to hiring managers. Analyze and report recruitment metrics to inform strategic decisions and continuous improvement. Supervisory Responsibility This position manages 3 employees in the Recruitment and Student Employment function and is responsible for the hiring and performance management of the employees in this department. Competencies, Education, Experience Demonstrated ability to lead recruitment initiatives that drive process improvements and deliver exceptional candidate experiences. Strong project management and organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities and searches simultaneously. Expertise in faculty, staff, and administrative recruitment strategies and best practices. Strong leadership, coaching, and team development skills. Proficiency in analyzing recruitment metrics, workforce trends, and hiring data to inform decision-making. Ability to build collaborative relationships with academic leaders, hiring managers, and search committees. Exceptional communication, influencing, and stakeholder management skills. Ability to execute at strategic and tactical levels in a fast-paced environment. Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and flexibility. Technological proficiency and ability to lead system implementations. Sound judgment and decision-making skills. Education and Experience: Bachelor’s degree required; master’s degree in a related field preferred. At least 7 years of experience with an emphasis in recruiting and employment activities, and 3-5 years of experience leading a recruitment team and/or function. Professional experience in the higher education sector a plus. Technologically proficient; experience with applicant tracking systems. Supplemental Information Work Environment This job operates in a professional office environment. This role routinely uses standard office equipment such as personal computers, printers, etc. Physical Demands While performing the duties of this job, the employee is occasionally required to stand, move about, sit, to carry work and use controls, reach with hands and arms, climb stairs, communicate. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work This is a full-time position. Days and hours of work are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. This role may be eligible for hybrid/remote work 1-2 days per week after a 3-month orientation period, with the understanding that the person in role will be required to report to campus for interviews, trainings, workshops, and other work-related meetings or events as determined by business needs or other circumstances. Travel Travel is primarily local and may include visits to the other NY campuses. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. St. John’s offers an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, employees can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstore, dining facilities, campus recreation, and sporting events. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
