    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5459982/executive-director-of-university-events": {
      "title": "Executive Director of University Events",
      "description": "Executive Director of University Events Salary $127,000.00 - $140,000.00 Annually Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Full-Time Administrator Job Number 202600040 Org Office of University Events Opening Date 08/25/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary The Executive Director serves as the University’s senior event leader, providing strategic direction, institutional governance, and executive oversight for all presidential-level and other high-impact University events, including commencement ceremonies and other major institutional events. Reporting to and closely aligned with the Vice President, Chief of Staff, and University Secretary, in the Office of the President, this role is responsible for setting vision, priorities, and standards for the University’s event portfolio while ensuring consistent, high-quality execution and protection of the University’s reputation. This position provides strong executive leadership over the event team, with a clear expectation of effective delegation, performance management, and accountability across all assigned staff and event portfolios. The Executive Director is ultimately accountable for outcomes, but achieves results by building capacity within the team, empowering leaders at all levels, and ensuring decisions are made at the appropriate level, rather than through personal control of day-to-day tactics. As the strategic lead for university events, the Executive Director operates at the portfolio and enterprise level, translating presidential and University priorities into cohesive event strategies, setting standards and protocols, managing risk, and guiding teams to deliver results within defined frameworks. This role requires the ability to move fluidly between strategy and execution, stepping in directly where necessary for the most sensitive or high-stakes events, while maintaining focus on long-term planning, organizational effectiveness, and continuous improvement. The Executive Director leads cross-functional collaboration with academic and administrative partners, exercises sound financial oversight of event budgets and resources, and serves as a trusted advisor to senior leadership. A core responsibility of the role is ensuring that institutional protocol, quality control, and risk assessment are embedded into all planning and execution, and that leaders are equipped with clear, timely, and decision-ready information. This position reflects a deliberate shift toward a tiered, standards-driven, governance-oriented, and data-informed model for managing University events. In its early phases, the Executive Director is responsible for building and operationalizing the governance framework, establishing clear roles, decision rights, and performance expectations, and strengthening the Office’s capacity to operate effectively without over-reliance on any single individual. The role is expected to lead at a strategic and enterprise level, setting direction, priorities, and standards; developing leaders within the team; and ensuring accountability for results. Execution is intentionally focused on institutional-level and high-stakes events, with the Executive Director engaging directly where executive judgment, visibility, or risk requires it, while otherwise ensuring work is appropriately delegated and managed. Central to this work is improving efficiency, enforcing portfolio discipline, and implementing meaningful measures of success to guide future investment, resource allocation, and decision-making. While ultimately accountable for outcomes, the Executive Director is expected to delegate execution responsibility to portfolio-owning leaders, intervening directly only when institutional risk, protocol exception, or executive visibility requires. Job Responsibilities Leadership • Serve as the primary point of coordination between the Office and the Office of the President. • Collaborate with the Vice President, Chief of Staff, and University Secretary to translate University and presidential priorities into a cohesive, intentional event portfolio. • Build a high-functioning leadership team and operational structure that enables the Office to perform effectively without over-reliance on the Executive Director for day-to-day tactical decision-making. • Provide direct leadership and oversight of all professionals within the Office. • Ensure teams are on track with planning, execution, timelines, and deliverables across all assigned event portfolios. • Set and enforce event priorities, sequencing, and scope decisions to balance institutional risk, capacity, and strategic value. • Coach, guide, and hold staff accountable for performance, quality, and adherence to institutional standards. • Reassign resources, step into execution roles, or provide hands-on support as needed to ensure successful outcomes. • Set annual priorities, goals, and operating plans aligned with the University’s Strategic Plan. Event Oversight & Execution • Maintain ultimate responsibility for the successful execution of all events owned by the Office. • Oversee event strategy and approve run-of-show development, scripting, guest experience, and institutional messaging for events. • Personally plan and execute select institutional and presidential-level events where executive oversight, sensitivity, visibility, or risk require direct involvement. • Ensure events are executed professionally, on time, within scope, and in alignment with University priorities and expectations. • Ensure the President’s Office receives timely, accurate, and decision-ready event information, including calendars, briefs, scripts, guest lists, protocol considerations, risk factors, and supporting materials. • Anticipate needs and proactively manage executive readiness for major events. Event Governance, Standards & Protocol • Ensure the Office is established as a Center of Excellence, and lead the implementation and ongoing operation of the University’s event governance model, including standards, protocols, and decision-making frameworks. • Partner closely with the Manager of Protocol to ensure institutional protocol, academic tradition, and ceremonial standards are consistently applied. • Serve as the final authority for protocol, standards, and exception decisions related to events. • Ensure that unit-led events subject to governance requirements comply with established institutional standards and expectations. • Identify and manage reputational, operational, and protocol-related risks associated with events, escalating issues as appropriate. • Oversee the documentation and preservation of key institutional records related to major events, honors, awards, and ceremonies. Measurement & Assessment • Ensure that all Office-owned events have clear objectives, success measures, and post-event assessments, and that results are actively used to inform decision-making and performance accountability. • Lead post-event evaluations to assess effectiveness, outcomes, and opportunities for improvement, and use a Stop/Start/Continue framework to determine which events should be expanded, refined, consolidated, or discontinued based on institutional value and performance. • Use outcomes and ROI metrics to guide strategic decisions, resource allocation, and long-term portfolio strategy. Operations • Drive operational efficiency by improving processes, staffing models, technology use, cross-training, and scalable resourcing approaches that reduce bottlenecks and support effective delegation. • Provide executive oversight of key vendors and external partners supporting events to ensure quality, performance, and alignment with institutional standards. • Oversee budgets, financial reporting, and resources associated with events. • Maintain a University-wide institutional event calendar and coordinate cross-unit dependencies. • Lead annual and multi-year planning for events, with attention to academic cycles, holidays, and institutional priorities. Committees • Serve as a member/chair of the institutional committees, including: o Commencement Committees o Honorary Degree and Commencement Speaker Committee o University Speaker Committee o President’s Society Selection Committee Perform other related duties as required in support of presidential and institutional priorities. Competencies, Education, Experience • Operates with executive presence, sound judgment, and discretion; serves as a trusted leader capable of setting priorities, making trade-offs, and driving strategy aligned with presidential and university goals. • Demonstrates a strong governance mindset with the authority and confidence to establish, enforce, and uphold institutional standards, protocols, and decision rights across a complex organization. • Understanding of academic tradition, institutional protocol, and reputational risk; serves as steward of ceremonial standards and quality assurance for high-visibility initiatives. • Exceptional diplomatic and interpersonal skills; able to lead through influence, navigate competing priorities, and manage senior-level stakeholders with clarity, trust, and credibility. • Proven ability to lead and coordinate complex, high-profile events with multiple internal and external stakeholders while maintaining accountability, timelines, and institutional alignment. • Experience defining KPIs, conducting post-event assessments, and using data to inform strategic planning, continuous improvement, portfolio decisions, and long-term resource allocation. • Demonstrated ability to evaluate financial, strategic, and operational return on investment to guide prioritization, budgeting, and portfolio optimization across institutional events. • Proven people leader with experience building, developing, and holding accountable high-performing teams; fosters clarity of roles, collaboration, and professional growth. • Strong written and verbal communicator, particularly at the executive-level; communicates with transparency, consistency, and institutional awareness. • Technologically adept and operationally fluent; able to translate strategic vision into executable plans while embracing innovation, new tools, and best practices that enhance quality, efficiency, and impact. Required Education and Experience • Bachelor’s degree required. • Minimum of 10 years of progressive leadership experience in higher education or a comparably complex, mission-driven organization. • Demonstrated leadership experience, including work with executive leadership, presidents, or equivalent governing bodies. • Demonstrated success leading high-visibility institutional initiatives. • Proven ability to manage teams, oversee execution, and deliver results in high-stakes environments. • Demonstrated experience supervising and developing professional staff. • Strong judgment, discretion, and comfort working closely with senior and executive leadership and leading across organizational boundaries. Preferred Education and Experience • Master’s degree preferred. • Experience with institutional events, protocol, governance, commencement, or major ceremonies. • Experience developing or applying performance measures, KPIs, or structured post-event evaluations. Supervisory Responsibility Yes Additional Eligibility Qualifications N/A Supplemental Information Work Environment This job operates in a professional office environment. This role uses standard office equipment such as personal computers, printers, etc. Physical Demands While performing the duties of this job, the employee is required to stand, move about, and communicate. The employee must lift or move up to 20-40 lbs. and perform moderate physical activity for event preparations and setups. Environmental Conditions N/A Position Type/Expected Hours of Work This is a full-time position in the office. Days and hours of work are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. in the office. This position requires long hours and weekend work for event preparation and on event days. Attendance at events, and evening and weekend work is required. Work hours may vary based on operational needs. Travel Local and tri-state travel is required for off-site events, site visits, and event-related errands and meetings. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. St. John’s offers an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, employees can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstore, dining facilities, campus recreation, and sporting events. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5472238/law-school-proctor": {
      "title": "Law School Proctor",
      "description": "Law School Proctor Salary $17.00 Hourly Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Per Diem Job Number 202600071 Org Law School Education Opening Date 09/24/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary Position Title: Examination Proctor Department: Law School Campus: Queens Position Type: Part Time – Temporary Rate Per Hour: $17.00 Job Summary: Law School proctors are responsible for proctoring students’ final exams. They play an integral role in ensuring the efficient administration of exams and are expected to act in a calm, professional manner at all times. Final exams take place in May and December (see below for the next available exam dates). Exams are not held on weekends. Proctors must be available to work most of the day's exams are being offered as well as attend an orientation session prior to the exam period. Job Responsibilities Responsibilities: • Supervise individuals and classrooms, according to strict guidelines, to maintain the integrity of Law School exams. • Clearly articulate test related information including instructions to groups. • Ensure students comply with the Honor Code and set test instructions throughout the duration of the exam. • Assist in passing out, collecting, and securing testing and related materials. • May serve as a relief (supervising the room while active proctor is on a break) for active proctors. • Perform other duties assigned by the supervisors. Supplemental Information Next Exam Dates/Times Available: Exam Dates: Tuesday, December 1st, 2026, through Friday, December 11th, 2026 Mandatory Orientation, Tuesday, November 10th, 2026 Proctors need to be available for at least 7 of the exam dates. Actual proctor work assignments will be based on the final exam schedule of the Law School. Shifts Available: Shift #1: 9:00 AM to 1:30 PM (end time approximate). Shift #2: 2:00 PM to 6:30 PM (end time approximate). *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5505692/fashion-studies-adjunct-professor": {
      "title": "Fashion Studies Adjunct Professor",
      "description": "Fashion Studies Adjunct Professor Salary See Position Description Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202600083 Org Administration and Economics Opening Date 10/05/2026 Description Benefits Questions St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary Job Summary Division/Department Information The Division of Administration & Economics houses the B.S. in Fashion Studies, Program the B.S. in Enterprise Regulation: Profit and Non-Profit, and the A.S. Program in Business Administration. The Fashion Studies Program provides students with knowledge, skills, and experiences to succeed in the industry. The program provides a comprehensive, in-depth, and global approach to studying the business aspects of the fashion industry. The core curriculum consists of courses in various areas of fashion, business, ethics, and sustainability. Foundational knowledge and skills in fashion studies are supported. The Enterprise Regulation: The Profit and Non-Profit program focus on the business industry’s fundamental competencies, which are vital for the field’s future managers. The core curriculum in the liberal arts will provide students with the broad knowledge and strong communication skills that today’s employers expect of well-educated professionals. Business Law knowledge will facilitate effective managers and facilitate effective managers while also cultivating creativity, leadership skills and entrepreneurial abilities that are in wide demand in the business arena. This program provides students with business law knowledge, skills, and experiences to succeed in the business industry. Internships are an integral part of our programs. Internships may be completed in New York and at one of the University’s global campuses, locations in Rome, Paris, and Limerick, Ireland. The St. John’s Community provides internship guidance, mentoring students and provides students with the tutelage to succeed in their chosen career. Job Responsibilities Responsibilities The Fashion Studies Program provides students with the knowledge skills and experience to succeed in the industry. The program provides a comprehensive-in-depth and global approach to studying the business aspects of the fashion industry. The core curriculum consists of courses in various areas of fashion business, ethics, and sustainability. Foundational knowledge and skills in fashion studies are supported. The adjunct professor should be familiar with the digital fashion process. The adjunct professor should be comfortable teaching various courses such as: Current Issues in Fashion, Color in Life and Fashion, Construction of Denim: Past, Present and Future, and Retailing just to name a few of the courses. Competencies, Education, Experience Qualifications All applicants must hold a master’s degree; industry experience is needed. Must demonstrate a proficiency in the field of Fashion. Supplemental Information Application Process Applicants need to submit their CV which needs to include their teaching experience and courses taught and their industry experience to the St. John’s University website at St John's adjunct faculty career page · Please send letter of application, resume and three letters of reference · Contact Dr. Almerinda Forte, Chair, fortea@stjohns.edu for questions regarding the position In compliance with NYC’s Pay Transparency Act, the salary range for this position is Adjunct Instructor $1,803 per contact hour, Adjunct Assistant $1,906 per contact hour, Adjunct Associate $2,009 per contact hour, and Adjunct Professor $2,215 per contact hour. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is not eligible for benefits. 01 Do you have a Master's degree? Yes No Required Question Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5506794/library-and-information-science-adjunct-faculty-professor": {
      "title": "Library and Information Science Adjunct Faculty Professor",
      "description": "Library and Information Science Adjunct Faculty Professor Salary See Position Description Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202600084 Org Library Science Opening Date 10/06/2026 Description Benefits Questions St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary Adjunct Faculty in Library and Information Science St. John’s College of Liberal Arts and Sciences Division of Library and Information Science Position description The Division of Library and Information Science at St. John’s University seeks an adjunct faculty member to teach graduate courses in library and information science. The immediate teaching need is for a Spring 2027 course addressing social justice in the information professions. Potential teaching areas include social justice, programs and services for diverse populations, public and academic librarianship, and related areas of library and information science. Job Responsibilities Responsibilities The adjunct faculty member will be responsible for teaching an assigned graduate course, facilitating student learning and engagement, evaluating student work, providing timely feedback, and communicating regularly with students. The instructor will follow established course and program learning objectives and University policies and procedures. Competencies, Education, Experience Qualifications Required qualifications Master’s degree in library and information science or a closely related field Demonstrated knowledge of social justice issues and programs and services for diverse populations relevant to the information professions Teaching or professional experience in library and information science Preferred qualifications Experience teaching graduate students Experience teaching in an online environment Experience using Canvas or a comparable learning management system for online instruction Supplemental Information Application process Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae or résumé to the Department Chair, Dr. James Vorbach ( vorbachj@stjohns.edu ), along with any other materials requested in the posting. Compensation For the 2026–2027 academic year, compensation for newly hired adjunct faculty ranges from $1,803 to $2,215 per credit hour, depending on adjunct rank. For a three-credit course, the corresponding compensation range is $5,409 to $6,645. Adjunct rank will be determined through the University’s appointment process. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is not eligible for benefits. 01 Do you have a Master's degree? Yes No Required Question Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5508970/part-time-laboratory-technician": {
      "title": "Part-Time Laboratory Technician",
      "description": "Part-Time Laboratory Technician Salary $22.00 - $25.00 Hourly Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Part-Time Staff Job Number 202600085 Org Grants and Research Opening Date 10/07/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary This part-time laboratory technician position supports an NIH R16-funded research program in the Department of Biological Sciences. The primary responsibility of this position is to perform Western blotting and related cell and molecular biology experiments and to generate high-quality, reproducible experimental data. The ideal candidate will have substantial hands-on experience with Western blotting and should be able to perform the technique independently, including sample preparation, SDS-PAGE, protein transfer, antibody incubation, imaging, data analysis, and troubleshooting. Experience with mammalian cell culture and other molecular and cellular biology techniques is also desirable. The technician will work closely with faculty and students and may assist in establishing standardized laboratory procedures and training laboratory members in Western blotting and related techniques. This position has no supervisory or leadership responsibilities Job Responsibilities · Independently perform Western blotting experiments, including protein sample preparation, SDS-PAGE, membrane transfer, blocking, primary and secondary antibody incubation, washing, imaging, and quantitative analysis. · Optimize and troubleshoot Western blotting conditions to obtain reproducible, publication-quality data. · Maintain detailed experimental records, including sample information, experimental conditions, raw images, and quantitative results. · Perform mammalian cell culture and prepare cell lysates for biochemical analysis. · Assist with routine molecular and cellular biology experiments related to the NIH-funded research project. · Establish and follow standardized Western blotting procedures to improve experimental reproducibility within the laboratory. · Assist faculty and students with Western blotting techniques and troubleshooting as needed. · Prepare laboratory reagents, buffers, and stocks required for Western blotting and related experiments. · Maintain and appropriately use laboratory equipment related to protein analysis and cell culture. · Follow University laboratory safety, biosafety, and research compliance requirements. · Communicate experimental progress, technical problems, and results clearly with the Principal Investigator and other research team members. Competencies, Education, Experience Required Education and Experience: · Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and relevant hands-on laboratory experience. · Demonstrated hands-on experience performing Western blotting independently. · Demonstrated ability to follow experimental protocols accurately and generate reproducible laboratory results. Preferred Education and Experience: · Bachelor’s degree in Biology, Biochemistry, Molecular Biology, Chemistry, Biotechnology, or a related scientific field. · Previous research experience in an academic, biotechnology, pharmaceutical, or biomedical research laboratory. · Substantial hands-on experience with Western blotting and protein analysis. · Experience with mammalian cell culture and related molecular or cellular biology techniques. Required Qualifications and Skills · Western blotting proficiency: Demonstrated hands-on experience performing Western blotting independently and producing consistent, high-quality results. · Western blot troubleshooting: Ability to identify and correct common problems involving sample preparation, electrophoresis, protein transfer, antibody conditions, background, signal intensity, and imaging. · Experimental reproducibility: Ability to follow standardized procedures carefully and reproduce experimental results across independent experiments. · Laboratory technique: Strong pipetting, solution preparation, sample handling, and general laboratory skills. · Experimental documentation: Ability to maintain accurate and organized laboratory records and appropriately document raw experimental data. · Attention to detail: High level of accuracy in sample preparation, reagent preparation, experimental execution, and recordkeeping. · Problem solving: Ability to recognize technical problems and independently identify reasonable troubleshooting approaches. · Communication and teamwork: Ability to communicate effectively and work collaboratively with faculty, graduate students, and undergraduate researchers. · Safety awareness: Understanding of and adherence to laboratory safety and biosafety procedures. Preferred Qualifications and Skills · Experience performing Western blotting in a biomedical research laboratory, particularly where Western blot data were used for research presentations, manuscripts, or publications. · Experience with mammalian cell culture. · Experience preparing protein lysates and performing protein quantification assays. · Experience with fluorescence-based Western blot imaging and quantitative analysis. · Experience with LI-COR Odyssey or similar fluorescence imaging systems. · Familiarity with Image Studio or other software used for Western blot quantification. · Experience with molecular and cellular biology techniques such as fluorescence microscopy, transfection, or cell-based assays. · Experience training or assisting other laboratory personnel with Western blotting techniques. Supervisory Responsibility Not applicable. The position may provide technical guidance and hands-on assistance to students performing Western blotting and related laboratory procedures but does not have formal supervisory responsibility. Additional Eligibility Qualifications · Ability to complete mandatory University laboratory safety and compliance training. · Ability to work independently after appropriate orientation to laboratory-specific procedures. · Commitment to accurate recordkeeping, research integrity, and reproducible experimental practices. · Willingness to participate in a standardized technical skills assessment as part of the selection process, if necessary. Other Duties Please note that this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of all activities, duties, or responsibilities required of the employee. Duties and responsibilities may change as needed to meet the requirements of the NIH-funded research program and St. John’s University. Supplemental Information Work Environment This position operates in a biomedical research laboratory within the Department of Biological Sciences. The technician will routinely use laboratory equipment including micropipettes, centrifuges, electrophoresis and protein-transfer systems, fluorescence imaging equipment, biosafety cabinets, incubators, microscopes, balances, and computers. The work involves handling mammalian cell cultures, biological samples, antibodies, chemicals, and common laboratory reagents in accordance with institutional safety and biosafety requirements. Physical Demands While performing the duties of this job, the employee is regularly required to sit, stand, move within the laboratory, and use hands for precise laboratory procedures such as pipetting and sample handling. The employee may occasionally be required to lift or move laboratory supplies or equipment weighing up to 20 lbs. Close vision and the ability to distinguish and evaluate experimental samples and images are required. Environmental Conditions The work environment may involve routine exposure to: · Common laboratory chemicals, reagents, odors, or vapors. · Biological samples and mammalian cell cultures. · Laboratory equipment including centrifuges, electrophoresis systems, transfer equipment, imaging systems, incubators, and biosafety cabinets. · Sharps and other routine laboratory hazards. Appropriate personal protective equipment, safety training, and laboratory-specific training will be provided. The employee must comply with St. John’s University and applicable federal laboratory safety and biosafety requirements. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work This is a part-time position contingent upon the availability of NIH R16 grant funding. The anticipated workload is approximately 20 hours per week, with scheduling arranged during standard business hours Monday through Friday. Some flexibility in scheduling may be necessary because certain experimental procedures, including Western blotting and cell culture, may require work to be performed at specific times. Travel Not applicable. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5514953/part-time-library-assistant-weekends-academic-year-40-weeks": {
      "title": "Part-Time Library Assistant, Weekends (Academic year, 40 weeks)",
      "description": "Part-Time Library Assistant, Weekends (Academic year, 40 weeks) Salary $21.50 Hourly Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Part-Time Staff Job Number 202600093 Org Main Library, St Augustine Hall Opening Date 10/13/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary The Part-Time Library Assistant provides front-line customer service to our students, faculty, staff, and administrators. This position is a 40-week part-time position, based on the academic Fall/Spring calendar. Job Responsibilities Provide accurate, timely, student-centered information and service from the 3 rd Floor Service Desk in person, online or by telephone, supporting circulation, interlibrary loan, hold shelf, reserves, research, media, and related library services. Apply library policies, procedures, and workflows consistently to ensure accurate transactions, operational efficiency, and a positive experience for students and other library patrons. Identify and resolve patron questions and service issues in a timely manner, provide quality information and directions, and connect students to appropriate library resources and support. Support the effective operation of the circulation department by following established opening and closing procedures and maintaining an organized, welcoming, and productive service environment. Monitor study areas by taking hourly headcounts throughout the day. Supervise student workers as needed. This includes training, assigning, and prioritizing tasks, and reinforcing library policies and procedures. Maintain ongoing communication with department supervisor regarding operational issues, service challenges, and any other issues or concerns. Participate in library projects and initiatives that enhance operational efficiency, service quality, and the student experience. Adapt to changing workflow priorities and staffing needs while maintaining consistent high-quality service. Perform other duties as needed to support effective library operations and student success. Other Duties Please note that this job description is not designed to cover or contain a comprehensive listing of activities, duties or responsibilities that are required of the employee for this job. Duties, responsibilities, and activities may change at any time, with or without notice. with or without notice. Supervisory Responsibility N/A Competencies, Education, Experience Student Centered Service: Demonstrated commitment to creating a welcoming and supportive library experience that helps our library patrons obtain the resources and assistance they need to succeed. Library Systems Proficiency: Develop proficiency in all ILS/WMS functions, i.e., check in/checkout items, searching catalog for resources, locate course reserve materials etc. This includes the ability to locate library materials needed, diagnose and resolve patron issues in an efficient and timely manner in line with library policies. Attention to detail. Technology Skills: Strong computer skills and proficiency with Microsoft 365 applications including Outlook, Teams, Word, and Excel. Learn to use standard equipment – scanners, microfilm readers, etc. Operational Efficiency: Ability to manage multiple priorities, follow established workflows, identify inefficiencies, and complete tasks accurately and in a timely manner. Problem Solving: Ability to assess patron and operational issues, determine appropriate solutions, and resolve problems efficiently in accordance with library policies. Communication: Excellent interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills, with the ability to communicate clearly and professionally with library users. Teamwork and Independence: Ability to work effectively as part of a team while also using sound judgement and initiative while working independently. Adaptability: Ability to respond positively to changing priorities, workflows, staffing needs, and service demands. Continuous Learning: Willingness to learn skills and procedures pertaining to circulation, Course Reserves through formal and on-the-job training. Required Education and Experience: Some library experience or similar customer service experience. HS Diploma or associates degree. Must be able to work a flexible schedule as needed. Demonstrate reliable and predictable punctuality and attendance as required for the position. Preferred Education and Experience: Library public service experience. Bachelor’s degree. Supplemental Information Work Environment This position operates in an academic library setting, including the staffing of the Circulation Service Desk. Ability to operate office equipment. This position is required to use standard office equipment such as computers, printers, scanners, etc., and occasional use of microfilm or microfiche readers. Physical Demands Must be able to retrieve books from the library stacks and move a book truck holding items. Must be able to move frequently and lift materials for processing. While performing the duties of this job the employee is occasionally required to stand; move about; sit; carry work and use controls; reach with hands and arms; climb stairs, use the elevators; communicate. The employee must occasionally move up to 50 lbs. Specific vision abilities required by the employee include close vision, distance vision, color vision, peripheral vision, depth perception, and the ability to adjust focus. Environmental Conditions May be exposed to dust and mold typically found in libraries. Position Type/Expected Hours of Work Part- Time work schedule: Saturdays, 10am -6pm, Sundays 12pm-8pm, during the Academic year Must be able to work a flexible schedule as needed. This is a fully on-campus position. Travel N/A *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5447565/site-coordinator": {
      "title": "Site Coordinator",
      "description": "Site Coordinator Salary $66,300.00 Annually Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Full-Time Administrator Job Number 202600046 Org Grants and Research Opening Date 08/13/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity, and inclusion is at the heart of our mission.? As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community.? We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.?Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Department: ? Upward Bound Program? Title: ? Site Coordinator? Location: ? Long Island City HS, SJU campus for meetings, etc.? Upward Bound is a federal grant program designed to increase the number of low-income and first-generation students who are prepared to graduate from high school and enter and persist in post-secondary education. Upward Bound provides five-year grants partnerships to provide services at high poverty, underserved high schools. Upward Bound grantees serve an entire cohort of students beginning in the ninth grade and follow the cohort through their first year of college. Job Responsibilities Assist the Project Director with the implementation of goals and objectives Responsible for part-time staff supervision & coaching Provide oversight of UB teaching staff at partner school Aid with student/parent recruitment and outreach Schedule & facilitate project support services and activities at the partner school Coordinate program support with partners and SJU departments Monitor student attendance & provide direct support (i.e.- SEIJ academic/ college & career planning) Update project participants' files and the collection of project services data (i.e.- attendance, event sign-in sheets, registration forms etc.) Serve project participants in developing and achieving skill areas identified in his/her PLP (Personalized Leaming Plan) Support in project reporting and capturing of required data Participate regularly in various partner school committee meetings Participate in training and supervision meetings as required Competencies, Education, Experience Master's degree in counseling, education, psychology, social work, or other relevant discipline At least three years of experience in working with disadvantaged student populations in a secondary or higher education setting Experience in academic advising, tutoring, counseling, teaching, or student affairs; computer proficiency Knowledge of the college application process and financial aid policies and regulations Supplemental Information In compliance with NYC’s Pay Transparency Act, the annual salary for this position is $66,300. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer.?All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. St. John’s offers an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, employees can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstore, dining facilities, campus recreation, and sporting events. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/"
